{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019uydMvJ163ZaV6sczRHydA","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with small |s| to fix L₂\n     2→#\n     3→# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-3: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n     4→# but with s = -1/100 (instead of s = -1).\n     5→#\n     6→# KEY INSIGHT: The L₂ operator decomposes as:\n     7→#   L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n     8→# where (with α=1):\n     9→#   Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃)     ∝ |s|    (POSITIVE, since □(φψ̃) > 0 for double-log)\n    10→#   Term_D = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²        ∝ |s|³   (POSITIVE)\n    11→#   Term_E = s³λ³·(div terms)     ∝ |s|³   (MIXED sign, contains growing φ³ factor)\n    12→#\n    13→# For |s| << 1, the O(|s|) positive Term_B dominates the O(|s|³) mixed Term_E.\n    14→# The failure scale moves to R_crit ~ O(1/|s|²), which with |s| = 1/100 gives\n    15→# R_crit ~ 10⁴, meaning (1+t)(1+x) ~ 10^(10⁴·N/2), astronomically large.\n    16→#\n    17→# Crucially, on the actual domain [0,∞)×[0,T] with fixed T and c:\n    18→# - When z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 1: R is bounded by (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), so L₂ > 0\n    19→# - When z < 1: the large-R asymptotics give L₂ ~ (29 - 28z² - z)R > 0 since z < 1\n    20→# So L₂ > 0 everywhere on the problem domain for ANY fixed c, T > 0.\n    21→#\n    22→# Conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition are identical to Rounds 2-3\n    23→# (they don't depend on s at all), and were proven true there.\n    24→\n    25→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n    26→\n    27→subs_dict = {\n    28→    alpha : 1,\n    29→    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n    30→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n    31→}\n    32→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b1db25-cef4-4434-ae8d-fa0fa5f67b93","uuid":"3d19861c-ac92-4f95-b6b1-84e5ff47df77","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_4/candidate.py","content":"# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with small |s| to fix L₂\n#\n# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-3: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n# but with s = -1/100 (instead of s = -1).\n#\n# KEY INSIGHT: The L₂ operator decomposes as:\n#   L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n# where (with α=1):\n#   Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃)     ∝ |s|    (POSITIVE, since □(φψ̃) > 0 for double-log)\n#   Term_D = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²        ∝ |s|³   (POSITIVE)\n#   Term_E = s³λ³·(div terms)     ∝ |s|³   (MIXED sign, contains growing φ³ factor)\n#\n# For |s| << 1, the O(|s|) positive Term_B dominates the O(|s|³) mixed Term_E.\n# The failure scale moves to R_crit ~ O(1/|s|²), which with |s| = 1/100 gives\n# R_crit ~ 10⁴, meaning (1+t)(1+x) ~ 10^(10⁴·N/2), astronomically large.\n#\n# Crucially, on the actual domain [0,∞)×[0,T] with fixed T and c:\n# - When z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 1: R is bounded by (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), so L₂ > 0\n# - When z < 1: the large-R asymptotics give L₂ ~ (29 - 28z² - z)R > 0 since z < 1\n# So L₂ > 0 everywhere on the problem domain for ANY fixed c, T > 0.\n#\n# Conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition are identical to Rounds 2-3\n# (they don't depend on s at all), and were proven true there.\n\npsi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n\nsubs_dict = {\n    alpha : 1,\n    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n}\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
