{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01FjgpGDcb62DrQTANf16fFf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YR6DU8AVTsdT6YeXU9n9rC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"source /home/cyanz/miniforge3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate wave && python3 -c \"\nimport sympy as sp\n\nx, t = sp.symbols('x t', nonnegative=True, real=True)\nc = sp.symbols('c', positive=True)\nT_sym = sp.symbols('T', positive=True)\n\n# For the double-log ansatz psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x), alpha=1, lam=-1/20:\n# From the round 4 report, L2 numerator = (1+x)^4 * [A(z) - B(z)*R]\n# where z = c^2*(1+t)^2/(1+x)^2, R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10)\n# \n# With s = -sigma (sigma > 0):\n# The A and B coefficients come from the L2 formula.\n# The key terms in L2 are:\n# - Term1 = -(s/2)*lam^2 * square(phi * tilde_square(psi))  [O(sigma)]\n# - Term2 = -s^3*lam^4*phi^3*(tilde_square(psi))^2          [O(sigma^3)]\n# - Term3 = s^3*lam^3*(divergence terms)                     [O(sigma^3)]\n#\n# After normalizing by phi^3 and extracting common factors:\n# L2/phi^3 = (sigma/2)*lam^2*F1 + sigma^3*lam^4*F2 + sigma^3*lam^3*F3\n# where F1, F2, F3 are functions of x, t only (through psi derivatives)\n#\n# F1 involves -square(phi*tilde_square(psi))/phi^3, which contains phi^(-2) terms\n# Actually this is more complex. Let me do it numerically.\n\n# Actually, let me just directly verify by computing L2 symbolically for\n# s = -1/c^(1/5) and check if it's positive.\n\n# But first, note the problem statement says s, alpha, lambda may depend on c AND T.\n# The verify engine uses c as a positive symbol. Let me check if T is available...\n# Looking at the verify_engine, T is NOT defined as a symbol. \n# But the subs_dict values may depend on c (which is a positive SymPy symbol).\n# So we can make s depend on c but not T directly.\n# Actually, re-reading the instructions: 'subs_dict values may depend on c'\n# T is not injected as a symbol. So we can only depend on c.\n\n# With s depending only on c, we need:\n# (c*(1+T)^2)^(1/10) < C3 / sigma^2 for ALL T > 0\n# But if T is unbounded, this fails for any sigma(c).\n# \n# Wait -- re-reading the problem: 'for all (x,t) in [0,inf) x [0,T]'\n# So T is FIXED. The conditions must hold on [0,inf) x [0,T] for given c,T.\n# We need to find psi, s, alpha, lambda that may depend on c AND T.\n# \n# But the verify engine only has c as a symbol, not T. \n# Hmm, let me re-check the verify engine and see if T matters for L2.\n\n# Actually, the domain is [0,inf) x [0,T], so t ranges from 0 to T.\n# In the L2 condition, the maximum of R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) over the domain\n# is unbounded because x -> inf (even with t in [0,T]).\n# Wait, no: R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10). As x->inf with t fixed in [0,T],\n# R ~ x^(1/10) -> inf.\n# So R is unbounded on the domain [0,inf) x [0,T]!\n\n# That means the argument 'R_max bounded because t in [0,T]' only applies\n# in the z > 1 region. Let me re-examine.\n\n# z > 1 means c^2*(1+t)^2 > (1+x)^2, i.e., (1+x) < c*(1+t) <= c*(1+T)\n# So in the z > 1 region: (1+x) is bounded by c*(1+T)\n# R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) <= (c*(1+T)^2)^(1/10) = R_max(c,T)\n# This IS bounded for fixed c, T.\n\n# In the z <= 1 region: B(z) = (z-1)(28z+29) <= 0, so -B(z)*R >= 0\n# and N(z,R) = A(z) + |B(z)|*R > 0 always. So L2 > 0.\n\n# So the ONLY problematic region is z > 1, where R < R_max(c,T).\n# The condition becomes R_max(c,T) < f_min.\n# f_min ≈ 205437 when s = -1/100.\n# (c*(1+T)^2)^(1/10) < 205437\n# c*(1+T)^2 < 205437^10\n\n# For the SAME s, this fails for large c.\n# But if we set s = s(c,T), the f_min changes.\n\n# The question is: what is f_min as a function of s?\n# From the L2 analysis with general s:\n# The A(z) coefficients come from the O(s) term -> proportional to s\n# The B(z) coefficients come from the O(s^3) terms -> proportional to s^3\n# f_min = A/B ~ |s|/(|s|^3) = 1/s^2\n\n# More precisely, let me compute L2 for general sigma.\n# With psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x), alpha=1, lam=-1/20, s = -sigma:\n\nsigma = sp.symbols('sigma', positive=True)\nlam_val = sp.Rational(-1, 20)\npsi = -sp.ln(1+t) - sp.ln(1+x)\nphi = sp.exp(lam_val * psi)  # = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/20)\n\npsi_t = sp.diff(psi, t)   # -1/(1+t)\npsi_x = sp.diff(psi, x)   # -1/(1+x)\npsi_tt = sp.diff(psi, t, 2)  # 1/(1+t)^2\npsi_xx = sp.diff(psi, x, 2)  # 1/(1+x)^2\nbox_psi = psi_tt - c**2 * psi_xx  # 1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(1+x)^2\ntilde_box_psi = psi_t**2 - c**2 * psi_x**2  # 1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(1+x)^2\n\n# With alpha=1, both are the same!\nprint('box_psi =', sp.simplify(box_psi))\nprint('tilde_box_psi =', sp.simplify(tilde_box_psi))\nprint('They are equal:', sp.simplify(box_psi - tilde_box_psi) == 0)\nprint()\n\n# With alpha=1:\n# L2 = -(s/2)*lam^2 * square(phi*tilde_box_psi)\n#     - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*(tilde_box_psi)^2\n#     + s^3*lam^3*(dt(phi^3*tilde_box_psi*psi_t) + c^2*dx(phi^3*tilde_box_psi*psi_x))\n\n# Let me compute phi * tilde_box_psi\nphi_tilde = phi * tilde_box_psi\nphi_tilde_simplified = sp.simplify(phi_tilde)\nprint('phi * tilde_box_psi =', phi_tilde_simplified)\n\" 2>&1 | head -20\n","description":"Analyze L2 structure with general sigma"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1879,"cache_read_input_tokens":117546,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1879,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":24},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b1db25-cef4-4434-ae8d-fa0fa5f67b93","uuid":"00ea2d79-0698-4a93-968c-e95fe466e11e"}
