{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01ANVh4wVAQDg8tPVjh8MsQe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01SbeeXqqw736EomPTdQXxD5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Key mathematical insight: The L2 problem is structural.\n# Let me reconsider the approach. The problem says s, alpha, lam may depend on c AND T.\n# \n# The verify engine only injects c (positive symbol), not T. But maybe we can\n# use a symbolic T. Let me check...\n# Actually, looking at the engine code again:\n# mod.sp = sp   <- sympy is available\n# mod.c = c     <- c is available\n# So in the candidate file we can define our own symbols!\n# But the subs_dict must map to concrete values. If we introduce T, \n# it won't get substituted and the expressions will still contain T.\n# This won't work with the engine's numerical checking.\n\n# Let me think about fundamentally different ansatz structures.\n# \n# The L2 condition with alpha=1:\n# L2 = -(s/2)*lam^2*square(phi*tilde_sq(psi)) - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*(tilde_sq(psi))^2\n#     + s^3*lam^3*(div terms)\n#\n# The problematic term is the one with phi^3 = exp(3*lam*psi).\n# As lam*psi -> +inf (condition 2), phi^3 -> +inf.\n# The phi^3 terms are O(s^3), while the non-phi^3 terms from square(phi*tilde_sq) are O(s).\n#\n# For small |s|: the O(s) term dominates. But the O(s) term also involves phi factors.\n# Let me look at the square(phi*tilde_sq) term more carefully.\n#\n# square(phi*tilde_sq) = d^2/dt^2(phi*tilde_sq) - c^2*d^2/dx^2(phi*tilde_sq)\n# This involves d^2 phi/dx^2 * tilde_sq + 2*(d phi/dx)*(d tilde_sq/dx) + phi*(d^2 tilde_sq/dx^2)\n# and similar for t.\n# The phi terms here are O(phi) not O(phi^3), so they grow slower.\n#\n# The competition is:\n# O(|s|) * phi * (derivative terms)  vs  O(|s|^3) * phi^3 * (tilde_sq)^2\n#\n# For large phi (large lam*psi), phi^3 dominates phi, so O(|s|^3)*phi^3 eventually wins.\n# UNLESS tilde_sq decays fast enough to compensate.\n#\n# For the double-log ansatz: tilde_sq = 1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(1+x)^2 which is O(1)\n# and phi^3 grows. So phi^3*(tilde_sq)^2 grows.\n#\n# What if tilde_sq decays as 1/phi^k for some k > 2?\n# Then phi^3*(tilde_sq)^2 ~ phi^(3-2k) -> 0 if k > 3/2.\n#\n# tilde_sq = psi_t^2 - c^2*psi_x^2. For phi = exp(lam*psi), lam*psi -> +inf means\n# if lam < 0, psi -> -inf. \n# For psi ~ -x^a with a > 0: psi_x ~ -a*x^(a-1), (psi_x)^2 ~ a^2*x^(2a-2)\n# phi = exp(lam*psi) = exp(|lam|*x^a), so phi^3 = exp(3|lam|*x^a)\n# tilde_sq ~ c^2*a^2*x^(2a-2) (if spatial term dominates)\n# phi^3*(tilde_sq)^2 ~ exp(3|lam|*x^a)*c^4*a^4*x^(4a-4)\n# This blows up regardless. The exponential always wins.\n\n# WAIT. Let me reconsider. lam*psi -> +inf is condition 2. With phi = exp(lam*psi):\n# phi -> +inf as x -> +inf. So phi^3 -> +inf.\n# The ONLY way to control the phi^3 terms in L2 is if the coefficient (tilde_sq)^2\n# decays EXPONENTIALLY fast - i.e., tilde_sq = 0.\n# But tilde_sq = 0 kills everything.\n\n# OR: we need the O(s) term to also have phi^3 growth.\n# square(phi*tilde_sq) involves d^2(phi*tilde_sq)/dt^2 and d^2(phi*tilde_sq)/dx^2.\n# If phi ~ exp(lam*psi) and tilde_sq ~ O(1), then phi*tilde_sq ~ exp(lam*psi).\n# d^2/dx^2(phi*tilde_sq) ~ lam^2*(psi_x)^2*phi*tilde_sq + ... ~ O(phi)\n# So square(phi*tilde_sq) ~ O(phi), not O(phi^3).\n# The gap between O(phi) and O(phi^3) is O(phi^2) = exp(2*lam*psi), which grows to +inf.\n\n# This means for ANY ansatz where lam*psi -> +inf (condition 2),\n# the phi^3 terms in L2 will eventually dominate the phi terms,\n# making L2 negative if the phi^3 coefficient is negative for some direction.\n\n# The s^3*lam^3 divergence terms also contain phi^3, and they can be positive.\n# Let me check their structure more carefully.\n\n# s^3*lam^3*(d/dt(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_t) + c^2*d/dx(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_x))\n# = s^3*lam^3*(phi^3_t*tilde_sq*psi_t + phi^3*(tilde_sq)_t*psi_t + phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_tt\n#              + c^2*phi^3_x*tilde_sq*psi_x + c^2*phi^3*(tilde_sq)_x*psi_x + c^2*phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_xx)\n#\n# phi^3_t = 3*lam*psi_t*phi^3, phi^3_x = 3*lam*psi_x*phi^3\n# So div terms = phi^3 * [3*lam*tilde_sq*(psi_t^2 + c^2*psi_x^2) + ...]\n#              = phi^3 * [3*lam*tilde_sq*P + (tilde_sq_t*psi_t + c^2*tilde_sq_x*psi_x) + tilde_sq*(psi_tt + c^2*psi_xx)]\n# where P = psi_t^2 + c^2*psi_x^2\n\n# So the full phi^3 coefficient in L2 (O(s^3) terms) is:\n# s^3*lam^3*phi^3 * [3*lam*tilde_sq*P + div_stuff + (alpha-1)*tilde_sq*box_psi - lam*tilde_sq^2]\n# With alpha=1:\n# = s^3*lam^3*phi^3 * [3*lam*tilde_sq*P + div_stuff - lam*tilde_sq^2]\n# = s^3*lam^3*phi^3 * [lam*tilde_sq*(3P - tilde_sq) + div_stuff]\n# = s^3*lam^3*phi^3 * [lam*tilde_sq*(3(psi_t^2+c^2*psi_x^2) - (psi_t^2-c^2*psi_x^2)) + div_stuff]\n# = s^3*lam^3*phi^3 * [lam*tilde_sq*(2*psi_t^2 + 4*c^2*psi_x^2) + div_stuff]\n\n# Hmm this is getting complex. Let me try a COMPLETELY different approach.\n# What if we use alpha != 1? With alpha = 1, the (alpha-1) terms vanish, which kills\n# half of L2. Maybe with alpha != 1, the additional terms help.\n\n# Actually, let me try the approach from the Round 4 guidance: make s depend on c.\n# The engine has c as a positive symbol. We can set s = -1/c in subs_dict.\n# The question is whether the engine will handle this correctly.\n\n# With s = -1/c^k:\n# The O(s^3) phi^3 terms scale as 1/c^{3k}\n# The O(s) phi terms scale as 1/c^k\n# The overall L2 expression involves factors of c^2 and c^4 from psi derivatives.\n# \n# For the double-log ansatz with s=-1/c:\n# L2 ~ s*(term1) + s^3*(term2)\n# ~ (-1/c)*(term1) + (-1/c)^3*(term2)\n# ~ -term1/c + term2/c^3\n# For large c: -term1/c dominates if term1 > 0.\n\n# Let me just try it numerically!\nprint(\"Testing double-log with s = -1/c:\")\nprint()\n\ndef compute_L2_double_log_s_dep(x_val, t_val, c_val, s_val, lam_val=-0.05):\n    \"\"\"Compute L2 for psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) numerically.\"\"\"\n    N = -1/lam_val\n    w = 1 + x_val\n    v = 1 + t_val\n    \n    psi_val = -np.log(v) - np.log(w)\n    phi_val = np.exp(lam_val * psi_val)  # = (v*w)^(1/N)\n    \n    psi_t = -1/v\n    psi_x = -1/w\n    psi_tt = 1/v**2\n    psi_xx = 1/w**2\n    psi_tx = 0\n    \n    tilde_sq = psi_t**2 - c_val**2 * psi_x**2\n    box_psi = psi_tt - c_val**2 * psi_xx\n    \n    # With alpha=1, box_psi = tilde_sq for this ansatz\n    \n    # Build L2 numerically using the formula\n    # L2 = -(s/2)*lam^2 * square(phi*tilde_sq) \n    #     - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*tilde_sq^2\n    #     + s^3*lam^3*(div terms)\n    \n    # I'll use finite differences for the wave operator terms\n    h = 1e-6\n    \n    def phi_tilde_at(xx, tt):\n        psi_v = -np.log(1+tt) - np.log(1+xx)\n        phi_v = np.exp(lam_val * psi_v)\n        pt = -1/(1+tt)\n        px = -1/(1+xx)\n        ts = pt**2 - c_val**2*px**2\n        return phi_v * ts\n    \n    # square(phi*tilde_sq) via finite differences\n    f = phi_tilde_at\n    ftt = (f(x_val, t_val+h) - 2*f(x_val, t_val) + f(x_val, t_val-h)) / h**2\n    fxx = (f(x_val+h, t_val) - 2*f(x_val, t_val) + f(x_val-h, t_val)) / h**2\n    sq_phi_tilde = ftt - c_val**2 * fxx\n    \n    # Div terms: d/dt(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_t) + c^2*d/dx(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_x)\n    def div_t_at(xx, tt):\n        psi_v = -np.log(1+tt) - np.log(1+xx)\n        phi_v = np.exp(lam_val * psi_v)\n        pt = -1/(1+tt)\n        px = -1/(1+xx)\n        ts = pt**2 - c_val**2*px**2\n        return phi_v**3 * ts * pt\n    \n    def div_x_at(xx, tt):\n        psi_v = -np.log(1+tt) - np.log(1+xx)\n        phi_v = np.exp(lam_val * psi_v)\n        pt = -1/(1+tt)\n        px = -1/(1+xx)\n        ts = pt**2 - c_val**2*px**2\n        return phi_v**3 * ts * px\n    \n    div_t_dt = (div_t_at(x_val, t_val+h) - div_t_at(x_val, t_val-h)) / (2*h)\n    div_x_dx = (div_x_at(x_val+h, t_val) - div_x_at(x_val-h, t_val)) / (2*h)\n    div_terms = div_t_dt + c_val**2 * div_x_dx\n    \n    L2 = (-s_val/2 * lam_val**2 * sq_phi_tilde\n          - s_val**3 * lam_val**4 * phi_val**3 * tilde_sq**2\n          + s_val**3 * lam_val**3 * div_terms)\n    \n    return L2\n\n# Test with s = -1/c\nfor c_val in [0.5, 1, 2, 10, 100, 1000, 1e6, 1e10]:\n    s_val = -1/c_val\n    worst = float('inf')\n    worst_point = None\n    for t_val in [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000]:\n        for x_val in [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000]:\n            try:\n                L2 = compute_L2_double_log_s_dep(x_val, t_val, c_val, s_val)\n                if L2 < worst:\n                    worst = L2\n                    worst_point = (x_val, t_val)\n            except:\n                pass\n    sign = \"+\" if worst >= 0 else \"-\"\n    print(f\"  c={c_val:.0e}, s=-1/c={s_val:.2e}: min L2 = {worst:.3e} ({sign}) at {worst_point}\")\n\nprint()\nprint(\"Testing with s = -1/c^2:\")\nfor c_val in [0.5, 1, 2, 10, 100, 1000, 1e6]:\n    s_val = -1/c_val**2\n    worst = float('inf')\n    worst_point = None\n    for t_val in [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000]:\n        for x_val in [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000]:\n            try:\n                L2 = compute_L2_double_log_s_dep(x_val, t_val, c_val, s_val)\n                if L2 < worst:\n                    worst = L2\n                    worst_point = (x_val, t_val)\n            except:\n                pass\n    sign = \"+\" if worst >= 0 else \"-\"\n    print(f\"  c={c_val:.0e}, s=-1/c^2={s_val:.2e}: min L2 = {worst:.3e} ({sign}) at {worst_point}\")\nPYEOF\n","description":"Test double-log with s dependent on c","timeout":60000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1880,"cache_read_input_tokens":135820,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0b1db25-cef4-4434-ae8d-fa0fa5f67b93","uuid":"a583573f-4e63-475e-8397-8d8219b513ff"}
